eorist you  had  representatives of the far-right  movements and of the neo-nazi  movements as well but you also had  people uh  you know everyday people who worried  that their  fundamental rights were being um  trampled on because of the measures  being taken to try and tackle the  pandemic  now you also had signs that likened  wearing a mask to wearing a yellow star  during world war  ii or for example um there was a banner  showing  uh calling that the tag def high height  which means the day  of freedom except that this name is  actually the name of a nazi  propaganda film by eleni riefenstahl so  those signs were problematic  as well um people were calling for  angela merkel the chancellor  from chris for christian drawston who's  the main virologist  and the one who's beca who become quite  famous during that  uh pandemic for yen span to be locked up  so we see  a sea of angry people who demonstrated  on saturday in berlin  okay the politicians were very quick to  respond  to this especially considering the size  of the demonstration what were they  saying  well jen spann for example the health  ministers say that of course  people have a right to protest that's  not put in question however  if they do so without respecting social  distancing without wearing masks then  they endanger other people i've covered  for example the black lives matter all  throughout  june here in berlin and there was also  the question of how safe it was but  for example when there was the biggest  protest which gathered  15 000 people everybody was wearing a  mask so we really see two  sides of german society that are  clashing at the moment  okay not only clashing this one part of  german society  clearly rejecting guidelines that we've  been following since march  yeah exactly i think there's uh somehow  a lack of trust for one part of the  german society towards the government  and  its handling of the crisis there's also  signs of impatience and  perhaps it's more difficult to  understand here in germany because the  country  hasn't been hit so hard by the pandemic  not as hard as spain not as hard as  france or italy for example and  all things considered here in germany we  had a very mild lockdown we were still  able to go out  with a sea label to do a lot of things  that other countries couldn't yet  a lot of people feel that their  fundamental freedoms are being  threatened  okay not not as as severe as a lot of  places like like spain or france  um the lockdowns here the school's  reopening today in the german state of  mecklenburg four palm and other school  and other states that  to follow what are the guidelines for  for school reopenings  well today in uh mecca for pomeran uh  there's um a hundred fifty thousand  children  which will go back to school the plan is  to make it as normal as possible given  the circumstances they will have four to  five  teaching hours per day including music  classics sports  classes as well wearing a mask  won't be compulsory but children will be  asked to respect a  social distancing and when not possible  not to touch each other  in any case and also school years  will be divided so as to allow for one  class or one school year to be  quarantined  quarantined if there's an infection but  so as not to affect the whole school  okay so localizing also any possible  lockdowns  moving forward emmanuel thanks so much  for coming in on this today thank you  you
 

